{"ts": 1789779657.727941, "kind": "assistant_text", "text": "`evaluate` is deterministic (repeats return identical values), so all differences are real in-sample, and the question is purely how much will survive out-of-sample.\n\n**Choice:** `{0:+, 36:+, 37:\u2212}`, in-sample 1.267.\n\n**Calibrating my OOS forecast.** I should not predict 1.267 \u2014 that would ignore selection bias. Adjustments:\n\n- **Selection over ~60 specs.** I picked the max of many correlated evaluations. Even with no overfitting of the underlying signal, the argmax is biased upward. But the bias here is limited: features 0, 36, 37 were each identified as top performers *individually*, before any combination search. I'm not reaching into the noise tier.\n- **What's likely real.** Standalone Sharpes of 0.773 and 0.837 at n=5000 are ~3.5\u03c3 effects \u2014 those are almost certainly genuine. Feature 37 at \u22120.602 is ~2.7\u03c3, also likely real. The diversification gain from 0.84 \u2192 1.13 \u2192 1.27 reflects imperfect correlation between three real signals, which is the mechanism that *does* replicate.\n- **What's likely inflated.** The last increment (1.13 \u2192 1.27) is the most selection-contaminated, since I chose 37 as the best of ~25 third legs. I'd discount that increment heavily. The 6-variant at 1.232 is nearly tied, which tells me the specific choice between them is coin-flip noise \u2014 worth maybe 0.05-0.10 of shrinkage.\n- **Regime/structural drift.** OOS data may differ in ways beyond sampling noise. This argues for extra variance, and a bit more downward shift.\n\nNetting out: I shrink the in-sample 1.267 by roughly 25-30%, landing near **0.90**. The floor is reasonably firm \u2014 even if the third leg adds nothing OOS, the {0,36} pair alone should deliver ~0.9-1.0, and even a single feature gives ~0.8. The upside is capped near the in-sample value. For the SD, sampling error alone is ~0.22; adding selection and drift uncertainty, **0.30** captures a sensible range (roughly 0.3-1.5 at \u00b12\u03c3)."}
