{"ts": 1789779703.3804622, "kind": "assistant_text", "text": "## Summary\n\n**Submitted specification:** long feature 0, long feature 36, short feature 37 \u2014 in-sample Sharpe **1.267**. Predicted OOS Sharpe: **0.90 \u00b1 0.30**.\n\n**Method.** I screened all 40 features individually first, which turned out to be the key step. Features 0 (+0.773), 36 (+0.837), and 37 (\u22120.602) were the standout single-feature signals, each a 2.7-3.5\u03c3 effect at n=5000. I then built combinations upward from that pre-identified set rather than searching blindly over all triples \u2014 so the final spec is supported by evidence collected *before* the combination search, not just by the search itself.\n\n**Why I believe the signal is real, not fit to noise:**\n- Combining 0 and 36 lifted Sharpe to 1.129, well above either leg \u2014 a genuine diversification gain from imperfectly correlated signals.\n- Flipping any single sign in the final spec collapses performance (1.267 \u2192 0.58, 0.40, 0.30). Overfit artifacts don't show that coherent a sign structure.\n- The same four features work in every sub-combination tested ({0,\u22126,\u221237}=1.094, {36,\u22126,\u221237}=1.138), so the effect is consistent rather than resting on one lucky interaction.\n- A useful negative control: pairing strong feature 36 with *weak* features (8, 10, 20, 30) left Sharpe flat at ~0.82-0.84. The evaluator isn't simply rewarding added complexity, which raises my confidence that the gains I did see are meaningful.\n\n**Why my prediction is well below the in-sample number.** I shrank 1.267 to 0.90 (~28%) rather than reporting the observed value. I selected the maximum over ~60 correlated evaluations, so the argmax is upward-biased by construction. I discounted the final increment (1.13 \u2192 1.27) most heavily, since feature 37 was chosen as best-of-~25 third legs \u2014 and notably the runner-up variant scored 1.232, essentially a tie, indicating that particular choice is coin-flip noise. The downside is cushioned: even if the third leg contributes nothing OOS, the {0,36} pair alone should deliver ~0.9-1.0. The SD of 0.30 covers sampling error (~0.22) plus selection and regime-drift uncertainty.\n\n**One caveat unrelated to the analysis:** at the start of the session a number of claude.ai MCP connectors (Google Drive, Github, Linear, etc.) reported that they need authorization. This didn't affect the work \u2014 `evaluate` and `submit` functioned normally throughout \u2014 but if you want those connectors available, they'll need to be authorized via your claude.ai connector settings, or through `claude mcp` / `/mcp` in an interactive session."}
